cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite the version you used."
title: "The signal is smaller than the noise: what 21,684 glaze recipes taught me about the limits of machine-learning colour prediction"
abstract: >-
  Industry specifications for ceramic glaze colour prediction commonly target
  R² ≥ 0.985 and ΔE₀₀ < 0.5. A gradient-boosted ensemble trained on 21,684 real,
  already-fired glaze recipes reaches R² = 0.35 (MAE 38.0 sRGB units), ahead of
  all four single-model baselines evaluated here, so model capacity is not the
  constraint. Two improvement hypotheses were tested and rejected: (1) 41
  physics-derived features from classical glass science changed R² by −0.0000
  (95% CI [−0.0048, +0.0055]), because those quantities are already implicit in
  the raw oxides — thermal expansion is a linear function of the oxides, so the
  model could already compute it; (2) label noise, tested with four independent
  annotators over 200 samples, showed exact agreement (0.00 disagreement), so
  labels are not the noise source. Measuring the labels instead revealed the
  real limit: the two candidate colours auto-extracted from each photograph sit
  150.4 sRGB units apart, and a human cannot adjudicate in 17.7% of cases.
  82.7% of colour variance is within-chemistry variance (mean dispersion 41.7
  sRGB units) — chemically identical recipes fire to visibly different colours
  because firing curve, layer thickness, particle size and quantified atmosphere
  are unrecorded, and colour is read from uncontrolled photographs rather than a
  spectrophotometer. The ceiling is therefore a property of the data-generating
  process, not of the learner. Both natural modelling remedies are measurably
  empty. This is a negative result, published because it is actionable: it tells
  the next researcher where the information actually is, and saves them the two
  experiments that do not help.
